WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.000
GPTQ.

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:05.111
A smarter way to squeeze a model to four bits.

3
00:00:05.111 --> 00:00:08.533
It matters because the quality loss isn't spread evenly.

4
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:13.333
GPTQ is post-training quantization, done carefully.

5
00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:19.900
Instead of rounding every weight on its own, it works layer by layer, using a small calibration dataset.

6
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.585
It targets four bit GPU inference, and you almost never run it yourself.

7
00:00:24.585 --> 00:00:26.900
You download checkpoints from the Hub.

8
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.033
Here's the trick, shown on one layer.

9
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:32.958
We quantize the first column to four bits.

10
00:00:32.958 --> 00:00:34.833
That introduces a small error.

11
00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:39.179
Then the columns not yet quantized are adjusted to compensate.

12
00:00:39.179 --> 00:00:41.267
We repeat this column by column.

13
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:44.033
Zoom out to the whole workflow.

14
00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:46.700
Someone starts with the fp16 model.

15
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.767
They quantize it offline against about a hundred twenty eight calibration samples.

16
00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:53.800
The checkpoint is shared on the Hub.

17
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.233
You just load it for GPU inference.

18
00:00:57.433 --> 00:00:59.100
Here's how simple loading is.

19
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:04.167
We point at a pre-quantized GPTQ checkpoint and load it on the GPU.

20
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:09.233
Then we run our own eval prompts, because the error is tuned to the calibration data.

21
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:12.333
Here's the lesson in one incident.

22
00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:18.200
You swap a GPTQ checkpoint into Mission Control and casual questions look fine.

23
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.067
But your flight ops eval drops fifteen percent.

24
00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:27.957
The calibration set was web text, and your telemetry jargon is exactly what the quantizer never saw.

25
00:01:27.957 --> 00:01:30.000
Always eval on your own domain.

26
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.181
Let's recap.

27
00:01:32.181 --> 00:01:34.633
GPTQ quantizes layer by layer.

28
00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:37.200
Remaining weights absorb each step's error.

29
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.767
That error is tuned to the calibration set.

30
00:01:40.067 --> 00:01:42.960
So always eval on your own domain.

31
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.067
Next up: AWQ.
